title: A graph read by strangers must be a garden; mail is a stream built of its parts, and a message is done only when what it taught sits in a note addressed to no one.
pid: b2055646-5cba-4ddd-b46c-5299859d5c77
body: |2-
  Mike Caulfield's distinction, in "The Garden and the Stream" (2015,
  read whole): the garden is topology, notes placed among each other to
  be walked by anyone; the stream is serialization, utterances in time
  order, each understood only by one who knows what it answered, so
  "inhospitable to strangers". Every session here arrives a stranger,
  so what it must find is garden. Mail ('to Jeff', 'to Claude', the
  unintegrated list under a subscribee) is stream built of garden parts,
  and following is how the stream enters a graph: my territory is the
  gate between the two. Filing a message, by integrating it, moves it,
  but it is still a message, readable only with its thread. So a
  mission whose lesson is only in a letter has not finished: the lesson
  goes in a note a stranger would find by its title.
  He also quotes the rule of Bush's memex that skg's fork keeps: what
  you read you may change, "Not reply to, mind you. Change."
  https://hapgood.us/2015/10/17/the-garden-and-the-stream-a-technopastoral/
